      hu: "Girsu (más néven Telloh) sumer város, Irak déli részén, Lagastól északnyugatra. A város Lagas fő vallási központjaként szolgált, és Ningirsu istent tisztelték benne. Gudea uralkodása alatt épült fel a híres Eninnu templom, amely a sumer építészet egyik csúcspontja. A régészeti feltárások során előkerült Gudea számos szobra, valamint egy hatalmas agyagtábla-gyűjtemény, amely a sumer irodalom és adminisztráció fontos forrása. A város területe mintegy 100 hektár, és csatornahálózata biztosította a mezőgazdasági termelést. Girsu hanyatlása a Kr.e. 2. évezredben kezdődött, amikor a politikai központok áthelyeződtek.",
      ro: "Girsu (Telloh) a fost un oraș sumerian situat în sudul Irakului, la aproximativ 20 km nord-vest de Lagash. A fost centrul religios al statului Lagash, dedicat zeului Ningirsu. Cea mai importantă perioadă a fost sub domnia lui Gudea, care a construit templul Eninnu, decorat cu statui și reliefuri. Săpăturile au scos la iveală mii de tăblițe cuneiforme, inclusiv texte literare și administrative. Situl se întinde pe 100 de hectare și include rămășițele unui zigurat și palate. Girsu a fost un important centru de producție textilă și agricolă. Orașul a fost abandonat treptat după perioada babiloniană veche.",
      en: "Girsu (modern Telloh) was a key Sumerian city, serving as the religious capital of the Lagash city-state. Dedicated to the god Ningirsu, it flourished under King Gudea (c. 2144-2124 BC), who built the magnificent Eninnu temple. The city was a center of art, sculpture, and literature, with numerous statues of Gudea and inscribed stelae found here. Girsu's economy was based on irrigation agriculture and textile production. The site covers about 100 hectares and features a ziggurat, administrative buildings, and residential quarters. Excavations, notably by French archaeologist Ernest de Sarzec in the late 19th century, uncovered over 30,000 cuneiform tablets, providing a wealth of information about Sumerian society and religion. Girsu declined after the fall of the Third Dynasty of Ur and was eventually abandoned.",
