      en: "Isin (modern Bahriyat) was a Sumerian city that became the capital of an independent dynasty after the fall of the Ur III empire (c. 2000 BC). The First Dynasty of Isin ruled most of southern Mesopotamia for about 150 years, with kings such as Ishbi-Erra and Lipit-Ishtar, who issued law codes. The city was dedicated to the healing goddess Ninisina (later Gula). Isin's temple, known as Egalmah, was a center of medical learning. Excavations have revealed a large temple complex, residential areas, and thousands of clay tablets, including legal and economic records. The site covers about 100 hectares. Isin declined after the rise of Larsa and was eventually absorbed into the Babylonian Empire under Hammurabi.",
