      hu: "Szippar (Abu Habba) ókori sumer-babiloni város Irakban, Bagdadtól délnyugatra. A város a napisten Samas kultuszának központja volt, temploma az E-babbar a babiloni építészet egyik csúcspontja. Szippar hírneves volt az E-babbarban működő írnokiskolájáról, ahol a híres 'Szippari törvénykönyvet' is másolták. A város kereskedelmi csomópont volt a Perzsa-öböl és Mezopotámia északi része között. A régészeti lelőhely 150 hektárt fed le, és számos leletet, köztük agyagtáblákat és pecséthengereket találtak. Szippar a kasszu időkig lakott volt.",
      ro: "Sippar (Abu Habba) a fost un oraș sumerian și babilonian important, situat la 30 km sud-vest de Bagdad, în Irak. A fost centrul cultului zeului soarelui Shamash, cu templul E-babbar, unul dintre cele mai mari din Mesopotamia. Sippar a înflorit în perioada babiloniană veche și a fost un centru de învățământ, unde scribii copiau texte juridice și literare. Codul lui Hammurabi a fost găsit aici în 1901. Orașul a fost un nod comercial, legând Babilonul de rutele nordice. Situl se întinde pe 150 de hectare și include rămășițe de temple, palate și locuințe. Sippar a fost distrus de elamiți în 1155 î.Hr., dar reconstruit ulterior.",
      en: "Sippar (modern Abu Habba) was a major city in ancient Mesopotamia, located near present-day Baghdad. It was dedicated to the sun god Shamash and his temple E-babbar was one of the most important religious institutions in Babylonia. The city was famous for its scribal school, where many legal and literary texts were composed and copied, including the Code of Hammurabi, which was discovered at Sippar in 1901 by French archaeologists. Sippar also served as a commercial hub, connecting Babylon with trade routes to the north and the Persian Gulf. The site covers about 150 hectares and has been excavated extensively, revealing multiple occupation layers from the Early Dynastic through the Neo-Babylonian periods. Sippar's long history includes periods of destruction by Elamites and later rebuilding.",
