      en: "Eshnunna (modern Tell Asmar) was an ancient Sumerian city in the Diyala River region, northeast of Baghdad. It was a key commercial center controlling trade routes between Mesopotamia and Elam. The city is best known for the Law Code of Eshnunna, a collection of legal provisions dating to around 1930 BC, predating the Code of Hammurabi. Eshnunna's power peaked during the Isin-Larsa period, when it was ruled by a series of independent kings. Excavations by the Oriental Institute of Chicago uncovered a well-preserved palace, temples dedicated to deities like Tishpak, and numerous cuneiform tablets. The site covers 150 hectares. Eshnunna was destroyed by Hammurabi in 1760 BC and later rebuilt, but never regained its former prominence.",
