      en: "Wasit is a historical city in eastern Iraq, near the Iranian border. Founded in 702 CE by the Umayyad caliphate, it served as a provincial capital. The city was renowned for its grand mosques and palaces. It flourished under the Abbasids but declined after the Mongol invasion. The archaeological site today features remnants of city walls, a large souk, and a towering minaret. Wasit was a center of Islamic learning and trade, producing notable scholars. Its strategic location on the Tigris-Euphrates basin facilitated commerce.",
